[{:db/id 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-645fccc2-d171-5fdf-83e7-9f6a35f005d6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124402 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb R_3[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{ at^3-bt \\: | \\: a,b \\in\\mathbb{R} \\big\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124401 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R},$ and its subset $N$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $N$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $N$ is a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\nIV. $N$ is **not** a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-33331944-2261-5079-a111-5d0397fe6a4a" "question-7e86fe7b-82ad-5786-81ac-5ea87b6be543"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124301 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,3] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{ f \\in \\mathcal{C}[-1,3] \\: | \\: f(0)=-f(2) \\big\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-645fccc2-d171-5fdf-83e7-9f6a35f005d6" :question/id #uuid "645fccc2-d171-5fdf-83e7-9f6a35f005d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-107103" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $at-b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}t-{b}_{1}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}t-{b}_{2}$ are two polynomials from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}t-{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}t-{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})t-({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at-b\\in A$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(at-b) \\\\  & =(ka)t-(kb),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-69df5860-5124-59d0-9e37-9e55acc83e24"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-69df5860-5124-59d0-9e37-9e55acc83e24" :answer-field/id #uuid "69df5860-5124-59d0-9e37-9e55acc83e24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d3c0835e-5f1d-544e-a853-45dfc3915a23" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff6b6d03-54db-5749-ac52-1169cfe1be1b" "answer-d3c0835e-5f1d-544e-a853-45dfc3915a23" "answer-d31db710-ba28-5d15-bc4f-73b558920048" "answer-02490bd0-71b5-513c-b520-c0758ef51227" "answer-97e47326-ad4c-525e-b1f6-f379fa5508f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff6b6d03-54db-5749-ac52-1169cfe1be1b" :answer/id #uuid "ff6b6d03-54db-5749-ac52-1169cfe1be1b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3c0835e-5f1d-544e-a853-45dfc3915a23" :answer/id #uuid "d3c0835e-5f1d-544e-a853-45dfc3915a23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d31db710-ba28-5d15-bc4f-73b558920048" :answer/id #uuid "d31db710-ba28-5d15-bc4f-73b558920048" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02490bd0-71b5-513c-b520-c0758ef51227" :answer/id #uuid "02490bd0-71b5-513c-b520-c0758ef51227" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97e47326-ad4c-525e-b1f6-f379fa5508f3" :answer/id #uuid "97e47326-ad4c-525e-b1f6-f379fa5508f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186329726 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $B=\\{p(t)\\in {R}_{3}[t]\\,|\\,p(1)=1\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244019 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{5}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{5}+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360460 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{4}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $4,$ and its subset $B=\\{p(t)\\in {R}_{4}[t]\\,|\\,p(0)=1\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360459 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $B=\\{p(t)\\in {R}_{2}[t]\\,|\\,p(1)=2\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244021 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $C$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $C$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $C$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186329725 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $L$ containing all invertible $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $L$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244023 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$  and its subset $D$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of all its entries equals $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360464 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $H=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $H$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $H$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125155 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $D$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $D$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $1,$ then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}\n$$\n\n is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n1+1=2.\n$$\n\n This matrix does not belong to $D.$ So, $D$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $A$ is an element of $D$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $1,$ and $k=5\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\nkA\n$$\n\n is a matrix such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n5\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\n This matrix does not belong to $D.$ So, $D$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $D$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123530 :answer-field/correct 17592186123532 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360466 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $Q$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $Q$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $Q$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\nIV. $Q$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-33331944-2261-5079-a111-5d0397fe6a4a" :question/id #uuid "33331944-2261-5079-a111-5d0397fe6a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107146" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ and its subset $B=\\{f\\in C[-1,1]\\,|\\,f(1)\\ne f(-1)\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of $C[-1,1]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if  $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=-x,$ then $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are in $B,$ but\n\n$$\n(f+g)(x)=x-x=0\n$$\n\n for all $x\\in [0,1].$\nIn particular, \n\n$$\n(f+g)(1)=(f+g)(-1).\n$$\n\n So, $f+g\\notin B.$ Hence, $B$ is *not* closed under function addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $f(x)\\in B$ and $k=0,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(1) & =0\\cdot f(1) \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot f(-1) \\\\  & =(kf)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $kf\\notin B.$ Hence, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-50cbeb90-f349-5abb-ae1c-099b501ea736"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-50cbeb90-f349-5abb-ae1c-099b501ea736" :answer-field/id #uuid "50cbeb90-f349-5abb-ae1c-099b501ea736" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f2c27ec0-cc47-51bf-badb-c070af87e34b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f39e815a-9af1-5b99-ab72-d96f070a17af" "answer-ba1f709b-6e8f-51a1-8804-ab7f3827078d" "answer-418823f2-2b02-5914-8925-5d36dd36d6ce" "answer-77eb99e8-733d-5d18-a9f7-b73130eeaa2b" "answer-f2c27ec0-cc47-51bf-badb-c070af87e34b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f39e815a-9af1-5b99-ab72-d96f070a17af" :answer/id #uuid "f39e815a-9af1-5b99-ab72-d96f070a17af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba1f709b-6e8f-51a1-8804-ab7f3827078d" :answer/id #uuid "ba1f709b-6e8f-51a1-8804-ab7f3827078d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-418823f2-2b02-5914-8925-5d36dd36d6ce" :answer/id #uuid "418823f2-2b02-5914-8925-5d36dd36d6ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77eb99e8-733d-5d18-a9f7-b73130eeaa2b" :answer/id #uuid "77eb99e8-733d-5d18-a9f7-b73130eeaa2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2c27ec0-cc47-51bf-badb-c070af87e34b" :answer/id #uuid "f2c27ec0-cc47-51bf-badb-c070af87e34b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125157 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $D=\\{f\\in C[0,1]\\,|\\,f(0)=3\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $D$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $D$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\nIV. $D$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x),g(x)\\in D,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =3+3 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)\\in D$ and $k=5\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =(5f)(0) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15 \\\\  & \\ne 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $D$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123542 :answer-field/correct 17592186123546 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e86fe7b-82ad-5786-81ac-5ea87b6be543" :question/id #uuid "7e86fe7b-82ad-5786-81ac-5ea87b6be543" :question/math-academy-id "q-107145" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $G=\\{f\\in C[0,1]\\,|\\,f(0)\\ge 0\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $G$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $G$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $G$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\nIV. $G$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $f(x),g(x)\\in G,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $G$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in [0,1]$ and $k=-1\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =((-1)f)(0) \\\\  & =-f(0) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $G$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $G$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-17130b31-13ec-59ea-9907-9dc1bafa977c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-17130b31-13ec-59ea-9907-9dc1bafa977c" :answer-field/id #uuid "17130b31-13ec-59ea-9907-9dc1bafa977c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-81225a5a-eed1-5477-b5f6-02fdcd401991" :answer-field/choices ["answer-81225a5a-eed1-5477-b5f6-02fdcd401991" "answer-3829f03a-07c9-5578-b4ae-972b6075fce1" "answer-7e051e5b-7291-5e6a-843b-e237005f233c" "answer-40374cb8-c538-5fe0-94c9-123e108a0c2d" "answer-ac35a2f0-ba68-50d8-9b85-dec3524e6bf3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81225a5a-eed1-5477-b5f6-02fdcd401991" :answer/id #uuid "81225a5a-eed1-5477-b5f6-02fdcd401991" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3829f03a-07c9-5578-b4ae-972b6075fce1" :answer/id #uuid "3829f03a-07c9-5578-b4ae-972b6075fce1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II,   and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e051e5b-7291-5e6a-843b-e237005f233c" :answer/id #uuid "7e051e5b-7291-5e6a-843b-e237005f233c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II  and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40374cb8-c538-5fe0-94c9-123e108a0c2d" :answer/id #uuid "40374cb8-c538-5fe0-94c9-123e108a0c2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac35a2f0-ba68-50d8-9b85-dec3524e6bf3" :answer/id #uuid "ac35a2f0-ba68-50d8-9b85-dec3524e6bf3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360465 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $L$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $L$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125762 :tutorial/content "Given a vector space $V$, any subset $H$ of $V,$ which is itself a vector space, is called a **subspace** of $V$. \n\nFortunately, we do not need to check all vector space axioms to determine whether a subset of some vector space is a subspace. Instead, we only need to check the **subspace criterion**, which states the following:\n\n*A subset $H$ of a vector space $V$ is a subspace of $V$ if and only if $H$ is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication. That is to say, both of the following conditions hold:*\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ lie in $H$, then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ also lies in $H$. Formally,*\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in H.\n$$\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ lies in $H$, then $k \\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ also lies in $H$ for any scalar  $k$. Formally,* \n\n$$\nk \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\,\\mathbf{u} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  k\\cdot\\mathbf{u} \\in H.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_3[t]$ containing all polynomials of degree at most $3,$ along with the subset \n\n$$\nA = \\{ at^2 \\:|\\: a \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\n$$\n\nIs $A$ a subspace? Let's check the two subspace criterion conditions: \n\n- Suppose $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2$ and $p_2(t)=a_2 t^2$ are two polynomials from $A$. Summing the two polynomials, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{1}(t)+{p}_{2}(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{2})+({a}_{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $p_1(t)+p_2(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\n- If $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2 \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{p}_{1}(t) & =k({a}_{1}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{(k{a}_{1})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $kp_1(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $A$ is a subspace $\\mathbb{R}_3[t].$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124707 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125348 :step/math-academy-id 653353 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125595 :step/math-academy-id 653354 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125525 :step/math-academy-id 653355 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125524 :step/math-academy-id 653358 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125349 :step/math-academy-id 653361 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
